    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Supplemental Materials: Cues, Partisanship, and Willingness-to-Pay for Energy\"\nbibliography: references.bib\ncsl: american-political-science-association.csl\nfig-cap-location: top\nfontfamily: ebgaramond\nlink-bibliography: false\nformat:\n  html:\n    author:\n      - name: Matthew C. Nowlin\n        email: matthew.nowlin@uta.edu\n        corresponding: true\n        affiliations:\n          - name: University of Texas at Arlington\n            department: Department of Political Science\n    template-partials:\n      - title-metadata.html\n  pdf:\n    author:\n      - name: \"Matthew C. Nowlin^[Associate Professor, Department of Political Science, University of Texas at Arlington. Email: matthew.nowlin@uta.edu]\"\n    include-in-header:\n      text: |\n        \\usepackage{setspace}\n        \\usepackage{dcolumn}\n        \\usepackage{float}\n        \\usepackage{amsmath}\n        \\usepackage{ulem}\n        \\addtokomafont{disposition}{\\rmfamily}\n        \\normalem\n  docx:\n    author:\n      - name: \"Matthew C. Nowlin^[Associate Professor, Department of Political Science, University of Texas at Arlington. Email: matthew.nowlin@uta.edu]\"\n    reference-doc: custom-reference-doc.docx\n---\n\n\n\n# Robustness Check: Controlling for Concern about Energy Cost\n\nThe main text models the effect of cue condition, political identity, and their interaction on energy-source prioritization and willingness-to-pay (WTP), controlling for age, male, white, education, and income. Energy affordability was a salient concern during the period of data collection: respondents' mean concern about the cost of electricity was 7.88 on a 0 to 10 scale. Because this concern could plausibly confound cue and political-identity effects on prioritization and WTP, this appendix re-estimates every main-text model with concern about energy cost added as an additional control. As shown below, the coefficients, their significance, and the substantive conclusions are consistent with the main text.\n\nThe results for the energy source-priority scale are shown in @tbl-priority-ols-supp, parallel to the priority-scale table in the main text.\n\n\n::: {#tbl-priority-ols-supp .cell tbl-cap='Effect of cue condition, political beliefs, and their interaction on the energy source-priority scale, controlling for concern about energy cost (survey-weighted OLS; control condition and other/moderate identity as reference)'}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<!-- preamble start -->\n\n    <script src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/vincentarelbundock/tinytable@main/inst/tinytable.js\"></script>\n\n    <script>\n      // Create table-specific functions using external factory\n      const tableFns_n05m1i7soic017h7joh1 = TinyTable.createTableFunctions(\"tinytable_n05m1i7soic017h7joh1\");\n      // tinytable span after\n      window.addEventListener('load', function () {\n          var cellsToStyle = [\n            // tinytable style arrays after\n          { positions: [ { i: '31', j: 2 } ], css_id: 'tinytable_css_ubw0341jmg6z9sq0fr2j',}, \n          { positions: [ { i: '30', j: 2 } ], css_id: 'tinytable_css_w1zratbbx63w5cvwo74a',}, \n          { positions: [ { i: '1', j: 2 }, { i: '2', j: 2 }, { i: '3', j: 2 }, { i: '4', j: 2 }, { i: '5', j: 2 }, { i: '6', j: 2 }, { i: '7', j: 2 }, { i: '8', j: 2 }, { i: '9', j: 2 }, { i: '10', j: 2 }, { i: '11', j: 2 }, { i: '12', j: 2 }, { i: '13', j: 2 }, { i: '14', j: 2 }, { i: '15', j: 2 }, { i: '16', j: 2 }, { i: '17', j: 2 }, { i: '18', j: 2 }, { i: '19', j: 2 }, { i: '20', j: 2 }, { i: '21', j: 2 }, { i: '22', j: 2 }, { i: '23', j: 2 }, { i: '24', j: 2 }, { i: '25', j: 2 }, { i: '26', j: 2 }, { i: '27', j: 2 }, { i: '28', j: 2 }, { i: '29', j: 2 } ], css_id: 'tinytable_css_9fzpybfy3eq6vj4x67wo',}, \n          { positions: [ { i: '0', j: 2 } ], css_id: 'tinytable_css_pvlonrdqe2c2wvbcs7pg',}, \n          { positions: [ { i: '31', j: 1 } ], css_id: 'tinytable_css_sqo74fbwg9yg9hxfiik2',}, \n   