 data-col=\"1\">Num.Obs.</td>\n                  <td data-row=\"31\" data-col=\"2\">3113</td>\n                </tr>\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n    </div>\n<!-- hack to avoid NA insertion in last line -->\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![Predicted energy source-priority scale (1-7) by cue condition and political identity, controlling for concern about energy cost (survey-weighted OLS predictions with 95% CIs; concern about energy cost, age, male, white, education, and income held at their survey-weighted means)](supplemental-materials_files/figure-html/fig-priority-predicted-supp-1.png){#fig-priority-predicted-supp width=672}\n:::\n:::\n\n\nAs in the main text, conservative Republicans were more likely to prioritize fossil fuels (b = -1.22, *p* < .001) and liberal Democrats more likely to prioritize renewables (b = 0.65, *p* < .001). The interaction terms significant at *p* $\\leq$ .10 in this specification are:\n\n- Conservative Republican: b = -1.22, *p* = 0.000\n- Liberal Democrat: b = 0.65, *p* = 0.000\n- Climate cue × Liberal Democrat: b = 0.35, *p* = 0.042\n- Trump cue × Conservative Republican: b = -0.41, *p* = 0.076\n\nThe results for the WTP models are shown in @tbl-wtp-models-supp, parallel to the WTP models table in the main text.\n\n\n::: {#tbl-wtp-models-supp .cell tbl-cap='Effect of cue condition, political beliefs, and their interaction on willingness-to-pay for fossil fuels (any positive WTP, and amount among those with any positive WTP) and for renewables, controlling for concern about energy cost (survey-weighted logit/OLS; control condition and other/moderate identity as reference)'}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<!-- preamble start -->\n\n    <script src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/vincentarelbundock/tinytable@main/inst/tinytable.js\"></script>\n\n    <script>\n      // Create table-specific functions using external factory\n      const tableFns_xp88himcje2msf134wtv = TinyTable.createTableF